s mpmath Hardy-Z ground truth (2.746, 5.238, 8.418, 10.194, 11.920), max abs diff 0.049; q=35 χ_index=(1,1) t_max=30: max abs diff 0.062 — consistent with slot-3's 0.033 at q=11. **End-to-end π(x; q, a) timing 3-run median (q, factors, φ, K, t_single)**: (15, [3,5], 8, 50, 3.96ms), (35, [5,7], 24, 50, 5.25ms), (105, [3,5,7], 48, 50, 6.94ms), (105, [3,5,7], 48, 200, 38.4ms), (1001, [7,11,13], 720, 200, 166.6ms). **HEADLINE COMPARISON**: q=1009 (prime, φ=1008, slot-3, K=200): 186.3ms vs q=1001 (composite, φ=720, slot-4, K=200): 166.6ms — 11% wall-clock drop at q=1001 attributable mostly to smaller φ (29% reduction), NOT multi-axis-FFT-specific; per-character cost essentially unchanged because N_AFE depends on q (not φ) and is comparable between q=1001 and q=1009. **FFT vs DIRECT (no FFT sharing) within slot-4 pipeline**: q=105 K=50 N_AFE=283 n_t=218: FFT 5.09ms vs DIRECT 4.24ms = 0.83× (FFT loses, small composite q); q=1001 K=200 N_AFE=1872 n_t=823: FFT 145.24ms vs DIRECT 254.39ms = **1.75× FFT speedup** (vs slot-3's 2.04× at prime q=1009). **Multi-axis FFT preserves slot-3's structural lift in composite-q regime; does NOT introduce asymptotic gain.** Crossover q ∈ [200, 500] same as slot-2/slot-3 prime-q. Multi-axis FFT slightly underperforms single-axis at comparable q because numpy.fft.ifftn calls 1D FFT routines per axis; small per-axis transforms (e.g., shape (6, 10, 12) at q=1001) have proportionally higher constant overhead than one large 1D FFT (size 1008 at q=1009). **Falsifiers F4.1-F4.4 framing slot 5**: F4.1 multi-axis FFT does not give asymptotic improvement over slot-3 single-axis at composite q (constant-factor lift in same crossover regime); F4.2 end-to-end wall-clock dominated by AFE eval (N_AFE · n_t depends on q not φ, composite q inherits same AFE cost as nearby prime q); F4.3 multi-axis FFT does not shrink AFE truncation requirement (balanced N = √(qt/(2π)) is q-only); F4.4 cross-conductor amortisation across Q-family NOT attempted in slot 4 (slot