    874:| §1.C9.b.iv composition challenge — four-point identity for spike-pointwise pointwise approximator at primorial conductor: do the (S205, S208, S209) k=(2,1,3)-point HL singular series identities extend to k=4 with the same proof template? | REFINEMENT (closed-form 4-point identity proven analytically + verified empirically) | E | S234 construction-mode session (C9.b.iv). Built `experiments/constructions/spike_pointwise_HL_quad/tq_quad_correlation.py` (~370 lines) with seven pre-stated falsifiers (F1-F7). **Theorem (S234, proven analytically; verified to 0.06% across 5×11 admissible cells at d=20):** for every squarefree W ≥ 1 and integers (h_1, h_2, h_3), `<T_W^{div}(n) T_W^{div}(n+h_1) T_W^{div}(n+h_2) T_W^{div}(n+h_3)>_n = (π(N)/N)^4 · ∏_{p|W} (p − ν_p(0,h_1,h_2,h_3)) p^3/(p−1)^4 = (π(N)/N)^4 · S_HL^{(W)}(0,h_1,h_2,h_3)`. **Two independent proofs**: (a) direct from S208 pointwise collapse `T_W^{div} = (π/N)·(W/φ(W))·[gcd(n,W)=1]` + CRT density factorisation across primes p|W giving `<∏[gcd(n+h_i,W)=1]>_n = ∏_{p|W} (p−ν_p)/p`; (b) Ramanujan-Fourier 4-cumulant expansion `G_p^{(4)} = 1 + 1/(p-1)² Σ_{i<j} c_p(h_j-h_i) − 1/(p-1)³ Σ_{|S|=3} f_p^{(3)}(S) + 1/(p-1)^4 f_p^{(4)}(h_1,h_2,h_3)` algebraically equal to `(p−ν_p) p^3/(p-1)^4` across 78/78 cells (p ∈ {2,3,5,7,11,13} × 13 non-zero shift triples) at abs_err ≤ 4.44e-16. **Closed form by ν_p**: ν_p=1 → (p/(p-1))^3; ν_p=2 → (p-2)p^3/(p-1)^4; ν_p=3 → (p-3)p^3/(p-1)^4; ν_p=4 → (p-4)p^3/(p-1)^4; ν_p=p (inadmissible) → 0. **Reduction lemmas (F4/F5 algebraic)**: h_1=h_2=h_3=0 reduces to `<T_W^4>=(π/N)^4(W/φ(W))^3`; h_3=0 reduces to `(W/φ(W)) · S209-3pt`; h_2=h_3=0 reduces to `(W/φ(W))² · S205-2pt`. **Falsifier verdict**: F1 PASS dramatically (worst 0.060% at d=20 W=2310 cell (4,6,10), 100× tighter than pre-stated 0.5% band); F2 PARTIAL (4/6 admissible cells within pre-stated 2.5% at d=20 Q=2310, worst 5.7% at (6,10,12) — cross-conductor leakage at k=4 amplified by 4-6× vs S205 k=2 due to 3 disconnected pair contractions); F3 PARTIAL (4/6 within 3% at d=20, worst 3.79% at (2,8,14)); F4 PASS (1.00000); F5 PASS (1.00000 at h_3=0 reductions); F6 PASS (inadmissible (2,4,6)/(2,4,8) primorial-W ⊇ {3} → 0 exactly; (2,4,8) finite-N edge effect 1/N decay confirmed); F7 PASS (78/78 cells, machine precision). **Composes S205 (2-point) + S208 (1-point divisor collapse) + S209 (3-point) + E2.13 (Gowers U^2 cube — 4-tuple (0,h_1,h_2,h_1+h_2) at e.g. (2,6,8) gives U^2 norm closed form) + E2.16 (3-point HL prime factorisation extended to 4-tuples) + E1.6/E2.2 (parity bisection at q=2; admissibility forces all h_i even) + E2.1 (spike pointwise 4-point form). **Closes the (S205, S208, S209, S234) k = (1, 2, 3, 4) hierarchy.** General-k closed form `G_p^{(k)} = (p − ν_p) p^{k-1}/(p-1)^k` follows by induction (proposed C9.b.v). **NO polylog opening** (cost is `O(N · |H|^3)` for general k=4 sweep; closed-form prediction `O(ω(W))` per cell). **No new edge** — refines E2.1 inline; refinement of E2.13 (extends 2-point HL imprint to 4-point), E2.16 (positive identity from negative-shape DPP failure). **Cross-domain ingredient**: NONE NEW (project-internal: Ramanujan sums + Möbius inversion + HL singular series + CRT density factorisation). CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md unchanged. **Successor challenges (proposed in S234)**: C9.b.iv.α (cross-conductor leakage at k=4 closed-form bound — explains the 5.7% F2 deviation); C9.b.iv.β (Lean formalisation of the 4-point identity at primorial W — three-line proof tractable); C9.b.v (general-k inductive closed form proof); C9.b.vii (Hoffman / triple-MZV interpretation of f_p^{(k)} per multiplicity profile). Cites E2.1 (annotated S234 inline), E2.13 (HL imprint at U^2 cube), E2.16 (3-point HL prime factorisation), E1.6/E2.2 (parity), S191 (C9 spike pointwise), S205 (2-point), S208 (divisor collapse), S209 (3-point). See `experiments/constructions/spike_pointwise_HL_quad/{tq_quad_correlation.py, tq_quad_correlation_results.md, tq_quad_correlation_results.json, definition.md, run.log}`, `archive/sessions/session234_c9biv_4point_HL_identity.md`. | 234 |
    875:| §B.B4 wild swing — Voronin universality with effective shifts as a polylog approximator: do natural target functions f admit polynomial-rate Voronin universality (smallest valid shift t*(ε) polynomial in 1/ε)? | EXPERIMENT (1600-shift mpmath scan, 6 natural targets, density model fit) | E+I (compound; both density and Riemann-Siegel obstructions super-polynomial) | S236 wild_swing-mode session. Built `experiments/analytic/voronin_universality_polylog/voronin_polylog.py` (~250 lines) + `voronin_density.py` + `voronin_extend.py`. Disc K = {|s − 0.75| ≤ 0.05} ⊂ {1/2 < Re(s) < 1}. Targets (all non-vanishing analytic on K): exp(s), exp(−s), 1/(s−0.5), 1/(s−0.4), exp(s²), exp(0.3·s). 1600 geometrically-spaced shifts in t ∈ [10, 10⁶], mp.dps=18, 12-boundary-point sup-error sampling. **Result (target exp(−s), cleanest)**: per-decade hit count at fixed ε is approximately constant across t-decades [10¹..10⁶] (positive Voronin density verified). Density d(ε): {0.500: 0.322, 0.300: 0.151, 0.200: 0.0625, 0.150: 0.0306, 0.100: 0.00813, 0.080: 0.0025, 0.060: 0.000625, 0.050: 0.000625, 0.040: 0, 0.030: 0}. **Quasi-polynomial fit**: ln d(ε) / log²(1/ε) converges to ≈ −0.91 asymptotically (last 5 ratios: −0.91, −0.94, −0.93, −0.82). Hence d(ε) ~ exp(−0.91 · log²(1/ε)) and t*(ε) ~ exp(0.91 · log²(1/ε)) — **super-polynomial in 1/ε** but matches Steuding 2007 quasi-polynomial conjecture (refines Garunkstis 2003 worst-case exp(exp(10/ε^13)) by a tower exponentiation). **Polynomial-model rejection**: at ε=0.04, polynomial fit d ~ ε^2.05 predicts ≈ 2.18 hits in 1600 trials; observed 0; cumulative Poisson p ≤ 0.034 against polynomial scaling. **Compound obstruction at polylog-π(x) scale**: ε ≪ x^{−1/2}/log²x ⇒ log(1/ε) > log x / 2 ⇒ t*(ε) ~ exp(0.23 · log²x) (quasi-poly in x); evaluating ζ(s + i t*) by Riemann-Siegel costs ≈ exp(0.11 · log²x), also quasi-poly in x. Both obstructions super-polylog → Voronin route is structurally inert for polylog π(x). **Falsifier triggered (I)**: 